<% content_for :javascripts do %>
  <%= javascript_include_tag "api_docs", defer: true %>
<% end %>
<%= page_heading "Teaching with the Dog API", title: "Dog API for Teaching - Free Coding Exercises for the Classroom" %>
<% content_for :description do %>
  A free dog API for teaching: no key, CORS open, stable since 2016. Classroom
  coding exercises with starter code in JavaScript, Python and curl.
<% end %>
<% breadcrumb_structured_data([["Teaching with the Dog API", teach_path]]) %>
<% teach_structured_data %>
<% api = "https://dogapi.dog/api/v2" %>
<% breed_id = sample_ids["breed"] || "BREED_ID" %>

<p>
  A lot of the people who use this API are students. I see it linked from
  Canvas, Google Classroom, Moodle, Schoology, Colab notebooks, The Odin Project
  and at least one hiring take-home. This page is for whoever wrote those
  lessons, and for whoever is about to write the next one.
</p>

<p>
  It has <%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:breeds] %> breeds,
  <%= @counts[:groups] %> groups, <%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:facts] %>
  facts and <%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:images] %> pictures. Small enough
  to understand in an afternoon, big enough to need pagination.
</p>

<h2 id="stability">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#stability" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Why it's safe to build a course on
</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>It's been running since 2016</strong>, and I'm not going anywhere.</li>
  <li><strong>No key, no sign-up.</strong> Nobody spends the first twenty minutes of a lesson making an account, and nobody leaks a key in a public repo or a screen share.</li>
  <li><strong>CORS is open.</strong> <code>fetch()</code> works from a static page, a CodePen, a notebook or <code>localhost</code>, no proxy needed.</li>
  <li><strong>Versions don't vanish.</strong> When v2 shipped, v1 kept answering, and it still does. Your old slides keep working.</li>
  <li><strong>The rate limit is generous.</strong> <%= Rack::Attack::REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE %> requests per minute per IP, which a whole room sharing one connection stays well under. No monthly quota to run out of halfway through a term.</li>
  <li><strong>It's free</strong>, for classes, workshops, tutorials and videos, paid or not.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  <strong>My promise:</strong> I won't remove an endpoint or change what an
  existing endpoint returns in a way that breaks code written against it. New
  things get added; old things stay. If something ever has to go, it will be
  announced here well ahead of time, and the old version will keep answering
  for at least a full school year after that.
</p>

<h2 id="beginner">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#beginner" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Beginner
</h2>

<h3 id="random-fact">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#random-fact" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Fetch a random fact
</h3>

<p>
  <strong>Goal:</strong> make one request, read JSON, put a value on the page.
  <code>GET /api/v2/facts</code> returns one random fact; <code>?limit=</code>
  asks for up to 5. Then add a button that fetches another one.
</p>

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "JavaScript", language: "javascript", id: "teach-fact-js", content: <<~JS %>
  const res = await fetch("#{api}/facts");
  const json = await res.json();
  console.log(json.data[0].attributes.body);
JS

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "Python", id: "teach-fact-py", content: <<~PY %>
  import requests

  res = requests.get("#{api}/facts")
  print(res.json()["data"][0]["attributes"]["body"])
PY

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "curl", language: "shell", id: "teach-fact-curl", content: <<~SH %>
  curl "#{api}/facts?limit=3"
SH

<h3 id="random-dog">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#random-dog" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Show a random dog
</h3>

<p>
  <strong>Goal:</strong> see an API answer without writing any JavaScript.
  <code>GET /api/v2/breeds/image</code> redirects to a random dog picture, so an
  <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> can point straight at it. <code>?size=</code> takes
  <code>thumb</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>large</code> or
  <code>full</code>.
</p>

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "HTML", language: "html", id: "teach-img-html", content: <<~HTML %>
  <img src="#{api}/breeds/image?size=medium" alt="A random dog">
HTML

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "Python", id: "teach-img-py", content: <<~PY %>
  import requests

  res = requests.get("#{api}/breeds/image", allow_redirects=False)
  print(res.headers["Location"])
PY

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "curl", language: "shell", id: "teach-img-curl", content: <<~SH %>
  curl -sI "#{api}/breeds/image" | grep -i location
SH

<h2 id="intermediate">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#intermediate" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Intermediate
</h2>

<h3 id="paginate">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#paginate" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  List breeds, one page at a time
</h3>

<p>
  <strong>Goal:</strong> walk a paginated collection. <code>GET /api/v2/breeds</code>
  takes <code>page[number]</code> and <code>page[size]</code>, and the response
  carries <code>links.next</code> and <code>meta.pagination</code>. Follow
  <code>next</code> until it's gone. <code>filter[has_images]=true</code> keeps
  only breeds that have a picture.
</p>

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "JavaScript", language: "javascript", id: "teach-pages-js", content: <<~JS %>
  let url = "#{api}/breeds?page[size]=20&page[number]=1";
  while (url) {
    const json = await (await fetch(url)).json();
    json.data.forEach((breed) => console.log(breed.attributes.name));
    url = json.links.next;
  }
JS

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "Python", id: "teach-pages-py", content: <<~PY %>
  import requests

  url = "#{api}/breeds"
  params = {"page[size]": 20, "page[number]": 1}
  while url:
      json = requests.get(url, params=params).json()
      for breed in json["data"]:
          print(breed["attributes"]["name"])
      url, params = json["links"].get("next"), None
PY

<h3 id="relationships">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#relationships" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  A breed and its group
</h3>

<p>
  <strong>Goal:</strong> follow a relationship. <code>GET /api/v2/breeds/:id</code>
  returns a breed whose <code>relationships.group.data.id</code> points at
  <code>GET /api/v2/groups/:id</code>. Two requests, one join. Going the other
  way, <code>GET /api/v2/groups/:id?include=breeds</code> brings every breed of
  a group along in <code>included</code>. Ask for an id that doesn't exist too,
  and handle the 404 without breaking the page.
</p>

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "JavaScript", language: "javascript", id: "teach-rel-js", content: <<~JS %>
  const breed = (await (await fetch("#{api}/breeds/#{breed_id}")).json()).data;
  const groupId = breed.relationships.group.data.id;
  const group = (await (await fetch(`#{api}/groups/${groupId}`)).json()).data;
  console.log(`${breed.attributes.name} is in the ${group.attributes.name}`);
JS

<h2 id="projects">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#projects" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Project ideas
</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Breed quiz.</strong> Show a picture from <code>/api/v2/breeds/:id/image</code> and four breed names; the student guesses. <code>filter[has_images]=true</code> gives you the breeds worth asking about.</li>
  <li><strong>Flashcards.</strong> Breed name on one side, description, origin and life span on the other.</li>
  <li><strong>Comparison chart.</strong> Plot <code>life</code>, <code>male_weight</code> and <code>female_weight</code> across a group, then argue about what the ranges actually mean.</li>
  <li><strong>"Which group?" sorter.</strong> Drag breeds into their groups, checked against <code>/api/v2/groups?include=breeds</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Take-home interview.</strong> "Build a page that lists breeds with search and pagination, shows a detail view with the breed's group and picture, and handles loading and error states." Small enough for an evening, with enough edges to talk about.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  Every endpoint and parameter is in the <%= link_to "API v2 reference", api_v2_path %>,
  with a try-it button on each one.
</p>

<h2 id="share">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#share" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Share this with your class
</h2>

<p>
  If you link students to the site, use this link. Course platforms strip the
  referrer, so most classes show up to me as anonymous "direct" traffic. The
  tags on the end let me see that a class came by, and nothing more: no
  cookies, no accounts, nothing about your students.
</p>

<%= render "pages/api_code", label: "Link for your course", id: "teach-share-link", content: classroom_link %>

<p>
  And I'd really like to hear about your course: what you teach, who it's for,
  what broke. Email me at <%= mail_to "dev@dogapi.dog" %> or open an issue on
  <%= link_to "GitHub", "https://github.com/kinduff/dogapi.dog", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>.
  Knowing a class depends on something is the best reason I have to keep it
  exactly the way it is.
</p>
